WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.950
What condensed matter physics is not the same
thing as solid state physics.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:10.390
Solid state physics is the study of, say,
electrons in metals like insulators, semiconductors,

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:12.040
super conductors and so on.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:18.230
But what condensed matter physics is, is it
goes beyond the behavior of individual components

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:22.990
to see how they self organize in some kind
of collective state.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:27.439
In other words, the behavior of the collective
is much more important than the behavior of

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:28.909
the individual.

00:00:28.909 --> 00:00:32.830
An example that everyone's familiar with is
a flock of birds.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:34.300
Every bird is flying around.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.260
They, in principle, can fly in any direction.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.020
But in a flock they all fly together in some
cohesive way.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:44.790
Another example in physics is just atoms.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:49.260
If you push air or push water, your finger
just goes straight through it.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.910
But if I take this book and I push it, my
finger doesn't go through it.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:53.910
It just moves.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.580
I can transmit a force.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:00.350
That's because the atoms in it have self organized
into some collective state.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.980
Rather than just do their own thing, they
all move together.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:06.229
It's a kind of conspiracy of atoms, if you
will.

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:12.289
Now, turning to biology — the big puzzle
in early evolution is this: The planet started

00:01:12.289 --> 00:01:14.890
about 4.6 billion years ago.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:21.030
We know that the last universal common ancestor
of all life was about 3.8 billion years ago.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:25.130
That's the organism from which all life on
Earth is descended, or maybe a community of

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.290
organisms.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:34.189
Now, we know that because we can look at genes
in modern cells, genes that code for the machinery

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:36.009
that makes proteins.

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:42.109
That machinery we can trace back by undoing
the process of evolution, if you will, back

00:01:42.109 --> 00:01:44.689
to about 3.8 billion years ago.

00:01:44.689 --> 00:01:50.209
What that means is that the modern cell had
already evolved, more or less, with it's current

00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:51.950
complexity, 3.8 billion years ago.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.340
The planet is 4.6 billion years.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.749
So we went from nothing to the complexity
of the modern cell in less than a billion

00:01:58.749 --> 00:01:59.749
years.

00:01:59.749 --> 00:02:02.560
And after that evolution didn't do that much.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:09.550
So how is it possible that evolution started
off so fast and then later on made many evolutionary

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.650
changes but nothing fundamental to the architecture
of the cell?

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:18.319
Well the answer that we've proposed is that
it was, in fact, collective effects.

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:24.540
Today, genes are transmitted from one person
to the other, and that person has to be their

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:25.540
descendant.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.290
Everyone is familiar with a family tree.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:33.480
But what we discovered is that if we assumed
that early on in life genes are transmitted

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:37.920
between individuals, even if they're not related
— this is called horizontal gene transfer

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.330
— we get not a tree of life, but a network
of life.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:45.450
And the network grows exponentially fast.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:50.560
Just like how the Internet grew very quickly
in the mid-1990s, because you had a network

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:51.560
effect.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.640
All the computers in the world could talk
to one another.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.750
The understanding of condensed matter physics
enabled us to really understand how we could

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:05.739
model the collective behavior of organisms
that could transfer genes.

00:03:05.739 --> 00:03:11.530
What we able to show was that the properties
of the genetic code that emerged from that

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:15.230
would agree with what we see today.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:20.549
If we assumed just a regular tree of life
behavior — no network effect — we couldn't

00:03:20.549 --> 00:03:24.310
explain the speed of life, the speed at which
evolution occurred.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:28.920
We couldn't explain the fact that every organism
has the same genetic code.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:37.159
We couldn't explain the fact that the genetic
code is remarkably robust to ours.

00:03:37.159 --> 00:03:42.990
In short, then, condensed matter physics does
have applications in biology because biology

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.010
is all about collective effects.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:51.550
It's a wonderful testbed for understanding
ideas of condensed matter physics, but in

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:55.980
a more broader sense, the idea of strongly
interacting collective properties of matter.

